---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How to Care for a Hand-Woven Rug, Carpet or Wall Hanging | Harrow &amp; Thread"
  description="Vacuuming, rotation, spill management and professional cleaning for hand-woven wool rugs, wall-to-wall carpets and textile wall hangings. What to do, and what to avoid."
>
  <section class="section-pad">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="max-w-narrow mb-14 sm:mb-20">
        <p class="eyebrow text-graphite mb-6">Care</p>
        <h1 class="display text-3xl xs:text-4xl sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl text-ink m-0 mb-6">
          Looking after it.
        </h1>
        <p class="lede text-graphite m-0">
          Wool wants very little. Vacuum it, turn it twice a year, and it will outlast the room it was made for. You will receive care notes specific to your fibres on delivery.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="max-w-narrow">

      <!-- Regular Maintenance -->
      <h2 class="display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Regular maintenance</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 list-disc pl-5 mb-12 text-base text-graphite">
        <li>Vacuum your rug regularly, ideally once a week, using a vacuum without a beater bar for wool rugs.</li>
        <li>Rotate your rug 180 degrees every 6 months to ensure even wear and sun fading.</li>
        <li>Place a rug pad underneath to prevent slipping and add extra cushioning.</li>
        <li>Keep your rug away from direct sunlight to prevent colour fading over time.</li>
        <li>Remove shoes when walking on rugs to minimise dirt and wear.</li>
        <li>Trim any loose fibres or snags carefully; do not pull them.</li>
        <li>Use castor cups or furniture pads under heavy furniture to prevent crushing the pile.</li>
        <li>We do not recommend aftermarket protectors. They dull the dye, and wool already resists staining on its own.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Spill Management -->
      <h2 class="display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Spill management</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 list-disc pl-5 mb-12 text-base text-graphite">
        <li>Act immediately on spills: blot liquids with a clean, dry white cloth or paper towel.</li>
        <li>Never rub a spill, as this can push it deeper into the fibres and spread the stain.</li>
        <li>For solid spills, gently scrape up the material before blotting.</li>
        <li>Work from the outside of the stain inward to prevent it spreading.</li>
        <li>If needed, use a small amount of lukewarm water and mild soap, but always test on an inconspicuous area first.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Professional Cleaning -->
      <h2 class="display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Professional cleaning</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 list-disc pl-5 mb-12 text-base text-graphite">
        <li>Have it professionally cleaned every two to three years, sooner in a hallway or under a dining table.</li>
        <li>Use a cleaner who specialises in hand-woven or antique rugs. A standard carpet cleaner will damage the pile.</li>
        <li>Always inform the cleaner of your rug's specific materials and construction.</li>
        <li>Avoid steam cleaning or wet washing unless recommended by a specialist.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- Wall hangings -->
      <h2 class="display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">Wall hangings</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 list-disc pl-5 mb-12 text-base text-graphite">
        <li>Rotate the piece 180 degrees every 12 months if lighting conditions are uneven.</li>
        <li>Dust gently with a soft, dry cloth or a vacuum with an upholstery attachment on low suction.</li>
        <li>Keep out of direct sunlight to prevent colour fading over time.</li>
        <li>Do not hang in bathrooms, kitchens, or over heat sources. Moisture and heat will damage the fibres.</li>
        <li>Check the pole, brackets and wall fixings periodically. A large hand-woven piece is heavy.</li>
        <li>Ease the piece along the pole occasionally so the loops do not always carry the weight at the same points.</li>
      </ul>

      <!-- General -->
      <h2 class="display text-2xl sm:text-3xl text-ink mb-4">General advice</h2>
      <p class="prose-body text-graphite mb-4">
        Everything above applies to wool, which is what most commissions are made in. Silk blends and bamboo silk are more delicate and less forgiving of moisture, so the guidance you receive on delivery is specific to the fibres in your piece.
      </p>
      <p class="prose-body text-graphite">
        These pieces are made for indoors. If you have a specific room in mind (a bathroom, a conservatory, anywhere humid), ask us first and we will tell you whether it will hold up.
      </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>